## Creational vs Structural vs Behavioral

## Creational Patterns — Object Creation

*How and when objects come into existence.*

```typescript
// Singleton — one instance per app
export const supabase = createClient(url, key);

// Factory — delegate instantiation to a function/class
function createNotification(type: 'email' | 'sms', message: string): Notification {
  if (type === 'email') return new EmailNotification(message);
  return new SmsNotification(message);
}

// Builder — step-by-step construction of complex objects
const query = new QueryBuilder()
  .select('id', 'name')
  .from('users')
  .where('active', true)
  .limit(10)
  .build();
```

## Structural Patterns — Composition

*How objects and classes are assembled into larger structures.*

```typescript
// Adapter — makes incompatible interfaces work together
class LegacyUserAdapter {
  constructor(private legacy: LegacyUser) {}
  get fullName() { return `${this.legacy.first} ${this.legacy.last}`; }
}

// Facade — simplified interface over a complex subsystem
class AuthFacade {
  login(email: string, password: string) { /* hides 5 underlying steps */ }
}

// Decorator — adds behaviour without modifying the original
function withLogging<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(fn: T): T {
  return ((...args: any[]) => {
    console.log('calling', fn.name, args);
    return fn(...args);
  }) as T;
}
```

## Behavioral Patterns — Communication

*How objects interact and assign responsibilities.*

```typescript
// Observer — subscribe to events
window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize); // DOM Observer

// Strategy — swap algorithms at runtime
const sorter = new ProductList({ sortStrategy: alphabeticalSort });
sorter.setStrategy(priceAscendingSort);

// Command — encapsulate actions as objects (enables undo)
const command = new AddItemCommand(cart, item);
command.execute();
command.undo();
```

## Quick Reference

| Category | Concern | Key Patterns |
|---|---|---|
| Creational | Object creation | Singleton, Factory, Builder, Prototype |
| Structural | Composition | Adapter, Facade, Decorator, Proxy, Composite |
| Behavioral | Communication | Observer, Strategy, Command, State, Iterator |

## Observer Pattern

## Intent

Define a one-to-many relationship so that when one object changes state, its dependents are notified and updated automatically.

## Participants

```
Subject (Publisher)  — holds state, manages a list of observers
Observer (Subscriber) — receives notifications and reacts
```

## TypeScript Implementation

```typescript
interface Observer<T> {
  update(data: T): void;
}

class EventEmitter<T> {
  private observers: Observer<T>[] = [];

  subscribe(observer: Observer<T>) {
    this.observers.push(observer);
    return () => this.unsubscribe(observer); // returns unsubscribe fn
  }

  private unsubscribe(observer: Observer<T>) {
    this.observers = this.observers.filter(o => o !== observer);
  }

  emit(data: T) {
    this.observers.forEach(o => o.update(data));
  }
}

// Usage
const priceEmitter = new EventEmitter<number>();

const logger = { update: (price: number) => console.log('Price:', price) };
const cart = { update: (price: number) => recalculateTotal(price) };

priceEmitter.subscribe(logger);
priceEmitter.subscribe(cart);
priceEmitter.emit(49.99); // both logger and cart receive this
```

## In the DOM

```typescript
// addEventListener IS the Observer pattern
window.addEventListener('resize', () => recalcLayout());
document.querySelector('#btn')?.addEventListener('click', handleClick);
```

## In React / Zustand

```typescript
// Zustand store — components are observers
const useCartStore = create<CartState>((set) => ({
  items: [],
  addItem: (item) => set((state) => ({ items: [...state.items, item] })),
}));

// Component subscribes — re-renders when items change
function CartCount() {
  const count = useCartStore((state) => state.items.length);
  return <span>{count}</span>;
}
```

## In Node.js

```typescript
import { EventEmitter } from 'events';

const emitter = new EventEmitter();
emitter.on('data', (chunk) => process(chunk));
emitter.emit('data', buffer);
```

## When to Use

```
✅ One event, multiple independent listeners
✅ Decoupling the event source from handlers
✅ Real-time updates (WebSocket messages → multiple UI components)
❌ Simple callbacks — Observer adds unnecessary overhead for a single listener
```

## Factory Pattern

## Intent

Delegate object creation to a factory, hiding which concrete class is instantiated.

## Simple Factory

```typescript
type PaymentProvider = 'stripe' | 'paypal' | 'mock';

interface PaymentProcessor {
  charge(amount: number, currency: string): Promise<Receipt>;
}

function createPaymentProcessor(provider: PaymentProvider): PaymentProcessor {
  switch (provider) {
    case 'stripe':  return new StripeProcessor();
    case 'paypal':  return new PayPalProcessor();
    case 'mock':    return new MockProcessor();
    default:        throw new Error(`Unknown provider: ${provider}`);
  }
}

// Caller doesn't know or care which class it gets
const processor = createPaymentProcessor(
  process.env.PAYMENT_PROVIDER as PaymentProvider
);
await processor.charge(99.99, 'GBP');
```

## Factory Method Pattern

```typescript
abstract class Notification {
  send(message: string): void {
    const content = this.format(message); // calls factory method
    this.deliver(content);
  }

  protected abstract format(message: string): string; // factory method
  protected abstract deliver(content: string): void;
}

class EmailNotification extends Notification {
  protected format(message: string) { return `<html>${message}</html>`; }
  protected deliver(content: string) { sendEmail(content); }
}

class SmsNotification extends Notification {
  protected format(message: string) { return message.slice(0, 160); }
  protected deliver(content: string) { sendSms(content); }
}
```

## In React

```typescript
// React.createElement IS a factory
const element = React.createElement('button', { onClick: handleClick }, 'Click me');
// JSX is syntactic sugar for the above

// Component factory pattern — creates components based on config
function createFormField(type: 'text' | 'select' | 'checkbox') {
  const fieldMap = { text: TextField, select: SelectField, checkbox: CheckboxField };
  return fieldMap[type];
}

const FieldComponent = createFormField(fieldConfig.type);
return <FieldComponent {...fieldConfig} />;
```

## When to Use

```
✅ Multiple classes share an interface, which to use is decided at runtime
✅ Object creation is complex and should be centralised
✅ Testing — swap production impl for mock via the factory
❌ Only ever one concrete type — a factory is overkill
```

## Module Pattern

## The Problem It Solves

Before ES modules, JavaScript had no block scope or file scope. All variables were global — naming collisions were common in large apps.

## Classic IIFE Module (pre-ES6)

```javascript
// IIFE creates private scope
const CartModule = (function () {
  // Private — not accessible outside
  let _items = [];
  let _discount = 0;

  function _calculateTotal() {
    return _items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0) * (1 - _discount);
  }

  // Public API
  return {
    addItem(item) { _items.push(item); },
    setDiscount(d) { _discount = d; },
    getTotal() { return _calculateTotal(); },
  };
})();

CartModule.addItem({ price: 49.99 });
CartModule.getTotal(); // 49.99
console.log(CartModule._items); // undefined — private!
```

## Modern ES Module Pattern

```typescript
// cart.ts — every ES module has private scope by default
let items: CartItem[] = [];
let discount = 0;

function calculateTotal() {
  return items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0) * (1 - discount);
}

// Only exported symbols are public
export function addItem(item: CartItem) { items.push(item); }
export function setDiscount(d: number) { discount = d; }
export function getTotal() { return calculateTotal(); }
// calculateTotal and items are module-private
```

## Revealing Module Pattern

```typescript
function createAuthService() {
  // Everything private by default
  let currentUser: User | null = null;

  function validate(email: string) {
    return /^[^@]+@[^@]+\.[^@]+$/.test(email);
  }

  async function login(email: string, password: string) {
    if (!validate(email)) throw new Error('Invalid email');
    currentUser = await api.login(email, password);
  }

  function logout() { currentUser = null; }
  function getUser() { return currentUser; }

  // Reveal only public API
  return { login, logout, getUser };
}

export const authService = createAuthService();
```

## In React

```typescript
// Custom hooks are the React Module pattern
export function useCart() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState<CartItem[]>([]);

  // Private helper
  const calculateTotal = () => items.reduce((s, i) => s + i.price, 0);

  return {
    items,
    total: calculateTotal(),
    addItem: (item: CartItem) => setItems(prev => [...prev, item]),
  };
}
// calculateTotal is encapsulated — consumers never see it
```
